<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mark Twain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Samuel Langhorne Clemens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mark Twain, the pen name of Samuel Langhorne Clemens, was an American writer and humorist, author of The Adventures of Tom Sawyer and Adventures of Huckleberry Finn. A critic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> in his later years, he served as a vice-president of the American Anti-Imperialist League and wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-leopolds-soliloquy">King Leopold's Soliloquy</a> in 1905 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mark-twain.jpg" alt="Mark Twain" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.F. Bradley, New York · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mark_Twain_by_AF_Bradley_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Writers joined the campaign, including <b>Mark Twain</b> with King Leopold’s Soliloquy (1905) and Arthur Conan Doyle with The Crime of the Congo (1909), while Joseph Conrad’s Heart of Darkness, drawing on his own journey up the river in 1890, became one of the most widely read works on…</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…tune also carried many other words: the Civil War &quot;Marching Song of the First Arkansas&quot; of Black soldiers, <b>Mark Twain</b>'s satire on the Philippine–American War in 1901, the labour song &quot;Solidarity Forever&quot;, college fight songs, children's parodies, and football chants that began at Tottenham Hotspur in 1961 and…</blockquote><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Mark Twain</b>'s The Adventures of Tom Sawyer the congregation sings &quot;Old Hundred&quot; when the boys believed dead appear at their own funeral.</blockquote><a href="/songs/old-100th">Old 100th</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Samuel Langhorne Clemens</b> was born in Florida, Missouri.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-30">30 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Florida, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Redding, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.49,-91.79&amp;year=1883" aria-label="Florida on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2283 749 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mark Twain"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2733 1019h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2733 1019h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.49,-91.79&amp;year=1883">What was here in 1883? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let us endeavor so to live that when we come to die even the undertaker will be sorry.&#8221;</p><footer>Pudd'nhead Wilson (1894), chapter 6 &#183; original &#183; a motto from &quot;Pudd'nhead Wilson's Calendar&quot;, the novel's chapter headings &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/102/pg102-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It was wonderful to find America, but it would have been more wonderful to miss it.&#8221;</p><footer>Pudd'nhead Wilson (1894), conclusion &#183; original &#183; a motto from &quot;Pudd'nhead Wilson's Calendar&quot;, the novel's chapter headings &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/102/pg102-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Truth is stranger than fiction, but it is because Fiction is obliged to stick to possibilities; Truth isn’t.&#8221;</p><footer>Following the Equator (1897), chapter 15 &#183; original &#183; a motto from &quot;Pudd'nhead Wilson's New Calendar&quot; &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2895/pg2895-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Man is the Only Animal that Blushes. Or needs to.&#8221;</p><footer>Following the Equator (1897), chapter 27 &#183; original &#183; a motto from &quot;Pudd'nhead Wilson's New Calendar&quot; &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2895/pg2895-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Travel is fatal to prejudice, bigotry and narrow-mindedness, and many of our people need it sorely on these accounts. Broad, wholesome, charitable views of men and things can not be acquired by vegetating in one little corner of the earth all one’s lifetime.&#8221;</p><footer>The Innocents Abroad (1869), conclusion &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3176/pg3176-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Few things are harder to put up with than the annoyance of a good example.&#8221;</p><footer>Pudd'nhead Wilson (1894), chapter 19 &#183; original &#183; a motto from &quot;Pudd'nhead Wilson's Calendar&quot;, the novel's chapter headings &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/102/pg102-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
